---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav('do-i-need-a-mobile-app-or-is-a-mobile-friendly-website-enough')
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        Every business owner asks this eventually: build a proper app, or keep everything on a mobile-friendly
        website? The answer is usually the website, but there are a handful of situations where an app is genuinely
        the right call.
      </p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>How often will someone actually use it?</strong> If this is something people touch once or twice
            a year, like checking your hours or filling out a contact form, a website wins. If it is something
            people would open daily, an app starts to make sense.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Does it need to work without a reliable connection?</strong> Apps can cache data and function
            offline. A website generally cannot, unless it is built as a progressive web app. This matters most for
            people working in the field: tradespeople, warehouse staff, delivery drivers.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Does it need access to hardware a browser cannot reach?</strong> A PWA can use a camera and GPS
            reasonably well today. What it still cannot do, at least on iPhone, is reach Bluetooth peripherals, NFC,
            or USB accessories, and it has only limited access to sensors beyond the basics. If the product
            genuinely depends on that kind of hardware, a website cannot do it properly.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Do you need push notifications to bring people back?</strong> PWAs have been able to send push
            notifications on iPhone since iOS 16.4, so the capability itself is not the gap anymore. The gap is
            reach: someone first has to manually add the site to their home screen before they can even be asked to
            allow notifications, and once you account for that whole process, the realistic reachable audience for
            web push ends up far smaller than native, in the order of 10 to 15 times smaller by some estimates.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>How will people actually find it?</strong> Websites get found through search. Apps get found
            through app stores, or because someone was told to go and download it. A website delivers value the
            moment someone clicks a link. An app requires someone to decide to install something before they get
            anything at all, which is a much higher barrier.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Would someone actually want this on their home screen?</strong> People are genuinely reluctant to
            install an app for something minor. If you cannot clearly answer why someone would want your icon
            sitting on their phone, that is usually a sign you do not need one.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        There is also a middle ground worth knowing about: a progressive web app can offer an install-to-home-screen
        icon, offline caching, and now push notifications on most devices, without the cost or complexity of
        building and maintaining a full native app. What is left of the gap is deeper hardware access and how many
        people you can actually reach with notifications, not the basic feature set anymore. If you are weighing
        this up for your own business, that is exactly the kind of decision worth talking through before you commit
        to a build, through a short consulting conversation.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
